            <div xml:id="d369">

                <pb facs="0472" n="458" sameAs="frus:frus1947v05/pg458"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867.00/12–1947: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-12-19T15:00:00+02:00">December 19,
                            1947—3 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>962. Foreign Minister tells me in entire confidence Greek Government recently
                    been proposing to Turkish Government latter should suggest to US deliveries
                    military equipment under aid programs be expedited. Sadak said he appreciated
                    concern of Greeks in view serious problems they are facing, but Turkish
                    Government has every confidence US Government handling aid programs in most
                    appropriate effective manner. He said he was simply passing this on as of
                    possible interest.</p>
                <p>Sadak also said Greek Government has made suggestions to Turks looking to
                    combined military planning and eventual combined operations if future
                    developments warrant. Sadak said Turkey, of course, deeply interested in Greek
                    security situation since if Greece should come under Soviet control “half of
                    battle” for maintenance Turkish independence would be lost. However, in view
                    present instability Greek situation and desire avoid anything which might seem
                    provocative to others, Turkish Government replied to Greeks in vague and guarded
                    manner.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
